/workspace/shared/research/ladder_losses_es1.md-88-## Where the money gap opens
/workspace/shared/research/ladder_losses_es1.md-89-
/workspace/shared/research/ladder_losses_es1.md-90-Across g1’s 23 large losses, our money is still **+$3.2k at day 12** and **+$2.7k at day 15** on average; it turns **−$1.5k at day 24**, **−$3.5k at day 27**, and finishes **−$5.4k**. For g9’s four large losses, the analogous path is **+$1.5k at day 12**, **−$4.6k at day 18**, **−$8.2k at day 27**, final **−$7.0k**. This excludes an opening-only explanation and points to later production/market composition.
/workspace/shared/research/ladder_losses_es1.md-91-
/workspace/shared/research/ladder_losses_es1.md:92:For g1 large losses, opponents grossed $49.9k more revenue but also spent $44.5k more; they hired 9.0 more worker-days on average (only $1.0k more hire cost). The dominant gross-revenue difference is wheat (+$41.4k, +1,060 sold units), followed by tomato (+$3.7k) and carrot (+$2.5k). That is **not** a tape-safe target: the wheat gain is matched by purchase spending and represents a different commodity plan.
/workspace/shared/research/ladder_losses_es1.md-93-
/workspace/shared/research/ladder_losses_es1.md-94-For g9’s four large losses, the direction reverses: we grossed $27.5k more and spent $34.5k more. Opponents gained mostly strawberry (+$8.1k) and milk (+$4.8k) while we over-committed wheat (−$33.8k opponent-minus-us revenue). Again this is a macro allocation/cash-flow effect, not an omitted local operation.
